  <!-- ============================ 01 ============================ -->
  <section id="summary">
    <div class="opener"><span class="secnum">01</span><h2>Executive summary</h2></div>

    <div class="col">
      <p>The system is a custom contact centre that replaces off-the-shelf call centre software for a property-management support team. Residents call one number, choose a language, and are routed to an agent who speaks it. Agents take and place calls entirely in the browser — no desk phone, no softphone install.</p>
      <p>The product covers the full lifecycle of a support call: an inbound IVR with nine languages, language-aware round-robin routing, two-party conferences, warm transfer, hold, voicemail capture, a lightweight caller CRM, a bi-directional HubSpot sync for the commercial record, per-agent analytics, and a supervisor console that escalates calls nobody has answered.</p>
      <p>The interesting product work was not the happy path. It was everything that happens when a distributed telephony system disagrees with itself — and the case study below is organised around that: what we built, then the three classes of failure we had to design against, then a production incident that forced the largest single change to how the system handles failure.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="stats">
      <div><div class="stat-n">~5,000</div><div class="stat-k">Calls a month, measured</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">~230</div><div class="stat-k">Calls a working day, measured</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">~2 min</div><div class="stat-k">Average handled call</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">9</div><div class="stat-k">Languages in the IVR, each with its own routing queue</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <div class="callout deep">
        <span class="eyebrow">What that volume means for the routing model</span>
        <p>The call figures are taken from the telephony provider's own call logs and the platform's analytics in production, and the two cross-check: about 5,000 calls a month divides across roughly 22 working days into the 230 a day the dashboards report. Volume is quoted as a monthly average rather than a single month, because move-in and move-out seasonality moves it either side of that.</p>
        <p>The number that actually shaped the design is what falls out of it. Five thousand calls at a two-minute average is around 165 talk-hours a month — under eight hours a day spread across a multilingual team, which means agents are <em>not</em> on a call for most of their shift. That is not slack; it is the requirement. An agent has to be idle to be reachable in their language, so the scarce resource is a free Spanish speaker at 4pm rather than total utilisation.</p>
        <p>Which is why the routing model in section 04 optimises for per-language fairness rather than for keeping everyone maximally busy. A conventional occupancy target would have been the wrong objective for this product, and the volume is the evidence for that.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="eyebrow">The one-line version</span>
        <p>We shipped a browser-based multilingual contact centre, then spent the second half of the project converting silent failures into visible, recoverable ones — because in telephony a crash the user hears as &ldquo;dead air&rdquo; is worse than an error message.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 02 ============================ -->
  <section id="context">
    <div class="opener"><span class="secnum">02</span><h2>Context and the problem</h2></div>

    <div class="col">
      <h3>Why build rather than buy</h3>
      <p>Hosted contact centre products price per agent seat and assume a conventional support org. This team's requirements pulled in a different direction on three axes at once:</p>
    </div>

    <div class="full tw">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:24%">Requirement</th><th style="width:38%">Why off-the-shelf struggled</th><th>What we needed instead</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Language-first routing</span></td>
            <td>Skills-based routing exists, but tying a caller's IVR language choice to a per-language fairness queue is bespoke logic.</td>
            <td>A separate round-robin pointer per language, so a Spanish call never disadvantages the English queue.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Caller context at answer time</span></td>
            <td>Seat-priced products expect the caller to already exist in an external CRM, so resident context would have to be mirrored out of the product that owns the deposit account and back again on every call.</td>
            <td>Two layers, each holding what it owns: caller lookup by phone number, auto-created profiles and in-call notes beside the deposit account, with the commercial record synced out to HubSpot so sales and support see the same account without the call path depending on it.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Supervisor intervention</span></td>
            <td>Generic dashboards report on calls after the fact.</td>
            <td>A live console that flags a call ringing too long and lets a supervisor answer it or push it to voicemail mid-ring.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h3>The three users</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="full tw">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">User</th><th style="width:36%">Job to be done</th><th>How the product serves it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Resident</span><span class="s"><span class="chip">Caller</span></span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;Reach someone who speaks my language, without repeating myself or waiting forever.&rdquo;</td>
            <td>Nine-language IVR accepting both keypad and speech; two routing attempts; then voicemail rather than an endless ring.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Agent</span><span class="s"><span class="chip warn">Operator</span></span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;Take the next call, see who it is, handle it, hand it off if it isn't mine.&rdquo;</td>
            <td>Browser softphone, one availability toggle, caller profile and notes in the call modal, dial pad, hold, transfer, leave.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Supervisor</span><span class="s"><span class="chip deep">Admin</span></span></td>
            <td>&ldquo;Know that nothing is falling on the floor, and intervene when it is.&rdquo;</td>
            <td>Agent roster and language assignment, live queue positions, long-call alerts with answer / voicemail actions, alert history.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

