  <!-- ============================ 04 ============================ -->
  <section id="flows">
    <div class="opener"><span class="secnum">04</span><h2>The core flows</h2></div>

    <div class="col">
      <h3>Flow 1 — Inbound call to connected agent</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="full ladder">
      <div><span class="n">Step 1</span><span class="t">Caller dials</span><span class="s">Twilio posts the call to the app.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 2</span><span class="t">Language menu</span><span class="s">Digit or speech, nine options.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 3</span><span class="t">Round-robin pick</span><span class="s">Locked, per-language pointer.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 4</span><span class="t">Conference created</span><span class="s">Caller joins, agent is dialled.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 5</span><span class="t">Agent answers</span><span class="s">Two-party private conference.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="branch">
      <p><span class="lbl">No agent reachable</span>The app retries the search once, then records a voicemail rather than ringing indefinitely.</p>
      <p><span class="lbl">No pick-up in 25s</span>A timeout job re-routes to the next agent; on the second timeout the caller goes to voicemail.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <p>The routing decision is the product's most load-bearing piece of logic. It has to satisfy two things that pull against each other: <strong>fairness</strong> (agents should get roughly equal call volume) and <strong>reachability</strong> (never route to an agent who isn't really there). We resolved this by making the pointer advance only when a call is actually placed, and by checking three separate conditions before assigning — the agent is connected, has marked themselves available, and is not already on a call.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h3>Flow 2 — Outbound call from the browser</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="full ladder">
      <div><span class="n">Step 1</span><span class="t">Agent dials</span><span class="s">Number entered, country prefix.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 2</span><span class="t">Number validated</span><span class="s">Normalised to E.164.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 3</span><span class="t">Conference opened</span><span class="s">Agent's own outbound room.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 4</span><span class="t">Carrier leg dialled</span><span class="s">60s timeout, status callbacks.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">Step 5</span><span class="t">Agent joins</span><span class="s">Same conference, call live.</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="branch">
      <p><span class="lbl">Invalid number</span>The agent hears a spoken error instead of the call silently failing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="branch new">
      <p><span class="lbl">Carrier rejects the destination</span>The agent's call is marked failed, the agent is released back to available, and they hear why. <strong>This path did not exist before the June 2026 incident</strong> — see section 07.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h3>Flow 3 — Transfer, hold, and leave</h3>
      <p>Because every conversation is a conference rather than a direct bridge, transfer is a participant operation instead of a call teardown. The caller is placed on hold, the target agent is dialled into the same conference, and the original agent chooses whether to stay (three-way) or leave. A transfer can be cancelled while the second agent is still ringing, which returns the caller to the original agent rather than dropping them — the failure mode that a naive &ldquo;redirect the call&rdquo; implementation would produce.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h3>Flow 4 — The call nobody answered</h3>
    </div>

    <div class="full ladder">
      <div><span class="n">T = 0</span><span class="t">Call waiting</span><span class="s">Inbound leg, no live agent leg.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">T = 2 min</span><span class="t">Alert raised</span><span class="s">Pushed to online supervisors.</span></div>
      <div class="human"><span class="n">Supervisor</span><span class="t">Answer or divert</span><span class="s">Takes the call, or sends it to voicemail.</span><span class="m">human &middot; first</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">T = 3 min</span><span class="t">Auto voicemail</span><span class="s">If still unhandled, the system acts.</span></div>
      <div><span class="n">After</span><span class="t">Recorded &amp; audited</span><span class="s">Alert resolved, history kept.</span></div>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="eyebrow">Product judgement</span>
        <p>The system escalates to a human first and only acts unilaterally a minute later. Automating immediately would have been simpler, but it would have removed a supervisor's ability to rescue a call that was about to be answered.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

