  <!-- ============================ 06 ============================ -->
  <section id="state">
    <div class="opener"><span class="secnum">06</span><h2>The hard part: one agent, two sources of truth</h2></div>

    <div class="col">
      <p>An agent's availability has to be readable in single-digit milliseconds during routing, and it has to survive a server restart. Those two requirements point at different storage.</p>
      <p>We ended up with both: <strong>PostgreSQL</strong> holds the durable availability flag and the audit trail of every change; <strong>Redis</strong> holds the live operational state — connection count, availability, and an <code>on_call</code> marker — under a 30-second TTL, so an agent whose browser dies silently ages out of the routing pool instead of absorbing calls forever.</p>
      <p>Dual-writing state is a decision that buys speed and self-healing at the cost of a consistency problem you now own. Most of the second half of the project was spent paying that cost deliberately rather than accidentally. These were the failure modes and what each one forced us to build:</p>
    </div>

    <div class="full tw">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Failure mode</th><th style="width:34%">What the user experienced</th><th>What we built</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Double assignment</span><span class="s"><span class="chip live">Race</span></span></td>
            <td>Two simultaneous callers routed to the same agent; one heard ringing that went nowhere.</td>
            <td>Agent selection and the busy-marking now happen inside a single row lock on the language's pointer record, so the check and the claim cannot interleave.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Stranded busy agent</span><span class="s"><span class="chip live">Leak</span></span></td>
            <td>An agent stopped receiving calls indefinitely after a failed call setup.</td>
            <td>Explicit compensation: if dialling the agent into a conference fails or returns nothing, their busy state is rolled back and they return to the pool.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Out-of-order webhooks</span><span class="s"><span class="chip live">Ordering</span></span></td>
            <td>A live call was marked &ldquo;no answer&rdquo;; the agent's status flipped mid-conversation.</td>
            <td>A lifecycle rank per status. A callback that would move a call backwards — a stale <code>ringing</code> arriving after <code>in-progress</code> — is now rejected rather than applied.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Duplicate webhooks</span><span class="s"><span class="chip live">Idempotency</span></span></td>
            <td>Nothing visible to the caller, but Twilio saw repeated errors and stopped trusting our callback endpoint.</td>
            <td>Duplicate call identifiers are now treated as updates to the existing record instead of raising a uniqueness violation.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Silent browser death</span><span class="s"><span class="chip live">Liveness</span></span></td>
            <td>Calls routed into a closed laptop.</td>
            <td>TTL-based presence with a heartbeat, a delayed decrement on disconnect so a page refresh doesn't read as leaving, and reconnection before the platform's socket timeout.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Zombie active calls</span><span class="s"><span class="chip live">Staleness</span></span></td>
            <td>Supervisors saw alerts for calls that had ended hours or days earlier.</td>
            <td>The monitoring sweep now ignores <code>in-progress</code> records older than six hours, and logs how many it skipped rather than skipping them silently.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <div class="callout deep">
        <span class="eyebrow">The transferable lesson</span>
        <p>Every one of these was reported as a call-quality complaint, not as a bug. &ldquo;The call dropped&rdquo;, &ldquo;I stopped getting calls&rdquo;, &ldquo;it rang and nobody was there.&rdquo; Voice products compress a wide range of distinct technical failures into a single indistinguishable user experience — which means the instrumentation has to be good enough to tell them apart before you can prioritise between them. Building the separate online / available-in-DB / available-in-Redis indicators into the supervisor view was ultimately a product decision to make internal disagreement visible.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 07 ============================ -->
  <section id="incident">
    <div class="opener"><span class="secnum">07</span><h2>Incident deep dive: the dropped calls, June 2026</h2></div>

    <div class="col">
      <p>Agents reported roughly a minute of dead air on some outbound calls, followed by the call dropping. This is the clearest example of the pattern above, and it produced the single largest change to how the system handles failure.</p>
      <h3>What was actually happening</h3>
      <p>When an agent dialled a destination the telephony account was not permitted to call, the carrier rejected the outbound leg. The application did not handle that rejection — it raised an unhandled error and returned a server error to the caller's live browser session instead of returning valid call instructions. Three separate problems were tangled together:</p>
    </div>

    <div class="full tw">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:24%">Problem</th><th style="width:38%">Root cause</th><th>User-visible effect</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Unhandled carrier rejection</span></td>
            <td>Two distinct rejection codes on the outbound dial path were never rescued, so the active call path crashed.</td>
            <td>Dead air for up to the 60-second dial timeout, then a dropped call and inconsistent local state.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Non-idempotent callbacks</span></td>
            <td>A repeated call identifier hit a uniqueness validation and returned an error status.</td>
            <td>The telephony provider logged delivery warnings and retried; call records could diverge from reality.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Malformed callback URLs</span></td>
            <td>Phone numbers in E.164 form were embedded raw in query strings, so a leading <code>+</code> could decode as a space.</td>
            <td>The classifier could mistake our own caller ID for an external number, corrupting call direction.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <h3>The fix, and the principle behind it</h3>
      <p>The remediation was not &ldquo;make the carrier rejection stop happening&rdquo; — that is an account permissions matter outside the application. It was <strong>make a rejected call leg degrade into a handled outcome instead of a crash</strong>:</p>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="callout">
        <span class="eyebrow">What shipped</span>
        <ul>
          <li>The conference service now returns a structured failure result instead of letting a carrier error escape.</li>
          <li>The outbound path handles that result explicitly: it marks the call failed, releases the agent back to available, and returns valid instructions so the agent hears that the call could not be placed.</li>
          <li>Callback URLs are generated through framework helpers so phone numbers encode correctly.</li>
          <li>Duplicate callbacks update the existing record rather than raising.</li>
          <li>Legacy malformed numbers are normalised so they cannot fabricate a phantom external call.</li>
          <li>The monitoring sweep ignores very old <code>in-progress</code> records so historical data stops triggering live alerts.</li>
          <li>Focused regression tests were added for every one of the above paths.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <div class="callout live">
        <span class="eyebrow">Honest reckoning: what the incident review left open</span>
        <p>The written follow-up list is as important as the fix, and most of it is not code:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Account-level geography permissions still reject the destinations that triggered the incident. The app now fails gracefully; the calls still fail.</li>
          <li>A policy decision was deferred: whether to enable a whole calling region globally or gate it per team in the application. The review recommended app-level gating as the safer default — a product decision, not a technical one.</li>
          <li>The incident was detected by agents rather than by monitoring, which the review recorded as the real gap — see section 10.</li>
          <li>Existing stale production records need clearing, since the new six-hour cutoff prevents the symptom without cleaning the data.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <p>Writing the unresolved items down with owners and reasoning — rather than closing the incident when the code merged — is the part of this I would repeat unchanged.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 08 ============================ -->
  <section id="decisions">
    <div class="opener"><span class="secnum">08</span><h2>Decisions and trade-offs</h2></div>

    <div class="tw">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">Decision</th><th style="width:26%">Why</th><th style="width:26%">What it cost</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Conference for every call, not direct dial</span></td>
            <td>Transfer, hold, and supervisor entry all become participant operations instead of call teardowns.</td>
            <td>Two call legs per conversation; analytics must count distinct calls to avoid double-counting.</td>
            <td><span class="chip ok">Right call</span><span class="s">Made the entire transfer feature set possible.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Redis for live state alongside Postgres</span></td>
            <td>Routing reads must be fast, and TTLs give free self-healing when a browser dies.</td>
            <td>A real consistency problem, and most of the bugs in section 06.</td>
            <td><span class="chip warn">Right, underestimated</span><span class="s">Correct architecture; we under-budgeted the reconciliation work.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Per-language round-robin pointers</span></td>
            <td>One global queue would let a busy language starve a quiet one.</td>
            <td>More state to keep consistent; fairness is per-language, not global.</td>
            <td><span class="chip ok">Right call</span><span class="s">Directly serves the multilingual promise.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Escalate to a human before automating voicemail diversion</span></td>
            <td>A supervisor can rescue a call that is about to be answered; a timer cannot.</td>
            <td>An extra minute of caller wait in the worst case, plus alert-state machinery.</td>
            <td><span class="chip ok">Right call</span><span class="s">Automation as the fallback, not the first move.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Auto-create caller profiles on first contact</span></td>
            <td>Agents will not reliably create records; traffic will.</td>
            <td>Unnamed placeholder profiles accumulate and need curating.</td>
            <td><span class="chip ok">Right call</span><span class="s">Search history builds itself.</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><span class="t">Deploy latest revision at 100% traffic</span></td>
            <td>Simple, fast, and safe while consecutive versions stay compatible.</td>
            <td>Long-lived browser sessions can run old JavaScript against a new server.</td>
            <td><span class="chip crit">Conditional</span><span class="s">Acceptable only with the explicit compatibility rules we wrote down.</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <div class="callout crit">
        <span class="eyebrow">A deployment rule we had to learn the hard way</span>
        <p>Real-time apps break the usual assumption that a deploy is atomic. An agent's browser can hold a socket to the previous revision and keep running the previous version's JavaScript until it reconnects. We wrote that into explicit release rules: keep real-time message formats backward compatible for at least one deploy; never pair a destructive schema change with the code that depends on it; change protocols in three deploys (compatibility, switch, cleanup) rather than one.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 09 ============================ -->
  <section id="delivery">
    <div class="opener"><span class="secnum">09</span><h2>Delivery record</h2></div>

    <div class="stats">
      <div><div class="stat-n">18 mo</div><div class="stat-k">Dec 2024 to Jun 2026, continuous delivery</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">323</div><div class="stat-k">Commits across the 18 months</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">47</div><div class="stat-k">Schema migrations against a live product</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">28</div><div class="stat-k">Twilio webhook &amp; agent-action endpoints</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">13</div><div class="stat-k">Core domain tables behind the call model</div></div>
      <div><div class="stat-n">2</div><div class="stat-k">Environments — staging default, production gated</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="full tw">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:20%">Phase</th><th style="width:18%">When</th><th>Focus</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Foundation</span></td><td class="num">Dec 2024 – Feb 2025</td><td>Multi-agent round robin, analytics, and the first working call path — production calls inside three months.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Call control</span></td><td class="num">2025</td><td>Transfer logic, hang-up handling, dial pad, voicemail, recording, client notes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Reliability</span></td><td class="num">late 2025</td><td>Busy-state management, race conditions, reconnection, timeout behaviour, debug instrumentation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Scale &amp; ops</span></td><td class="num">early 2026</td><td>Background jobs moved onto managed task queues, caching, long-call alerting, staging Redis separation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><span class="t">Hardening</span></td><td class="num">May–Jun 2026</td><td>Status-callback ordering, token refresh, carrier-failure handling, and the incident review in section 07.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <p>The shape of that timeline is itself the finding. The product was carrying real calls inside three months; the fifteen months after that were spent making it survive telephony. Features dominated the first nine, reliability the last nine. For a voice product that ordering is close to unavoidable — you cannot discover which failure modes matter until real calls are flowing — but a future project would budget for it explicitly rather than treating it as overrun.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============================ 10 ============================ -->
  <section id="next">
    <div class="opener"><span class="secnum">10</span><h2>What I would do next</h2></div>

    <div class="col">
      <p>Ranked by the ratio of risk removed to effort spent. The first two come directly out of the incident review; the last three are hygiene findings from auditing the running application.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="full tw">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:5%">#</th><th style="width:28%">Action</th><th>Why it matters</th><th style="width:14%">Type</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">1</td>
            <td><span class="t">Alert on telephony failures</span></td>
            <td>The June incident was detected by agents, not monitoring. Carrier rejections, outbound server errors, non-2xx callbacks, and stranded busy agents should all page someone.</td>
            <td><span class="chip crit">Risk</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">2</td>
            <td><span class="t">Decide the calling-geography policy</span></td>
            <td>Enable regions globally or gate them per team in the app. Until this is decided, some legitimate calls still fail — gracefully, but they fail.</td>
            <td><span class="chip warn">Policy</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">3</td>
            <td><span class="t">Close the routing-logic coverage gap</span></td>
            <td>Tests concentrate on models and services; the busiest controller — the one handling every telephony webhook — has the thinnest coverage relative to its complexity.</td>
            <td><span class="chip">Quality</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">4</td>
            <td><span class="t">Remove the dead call-history routes</span></td>
            <td>Seven RESTful routes are declared for call records but only one action exists, so a call-history URL returns an application error. Either build the screen or drop the routes.</td>
            <td><span class="chip">Hygiene</span></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="rank">5</td>
            <td><span class="t">Reconcile state rather than heal by TTL</span></td>
            <td>TTL expiry is a good safety net but a blunt instrument. A periodic reconciler comparing live and durable agent state would turn silent divergence into a reported metric.</td>
            <td><span class="chip deep">Architecture</span></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="col" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <div class="callout deep">
        <span class="eyebrow">Note on outcome metrics</span>
        <p>The volume and duration figures in section 01 come from the telephony provider's call logs and the platform's production analytics. Everything else here — languages, endpoints, tables, migrations, the failure modes, the incident — is a property of the application and can be checked against it.</p>
        <p>What this document still does not have is the <em>before and after</em>. The three numbers that would turn section 06 from a well-argued story into a measured result are the missed-call rate either side of the reliability phase, the change in stranded-agent incidents, and handle time against the vendor product this replaced. All three are derivable from data that already exists; none of them are quoted here, because nobody has yet gone back and cut them. That is the honest gap, and it is a smaller one than it was.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

